==========================================================
WINDOW BENCHMARK
==========================================================

Use this as the first end-to-end benchmark:

USER:

"Create a parametric rectangular window.

Width = 1200 mm
Height = 1500 mm
Frame Width = 75 mm
Frame Depth = 100 mm
Glass Thickness = 10 mm."

EXPECTED ACTUAL REVIT RESULT:

Parameters:

- Width
- Height
- Frame Width
- Frame Depth
- Glass Thickness

Geometry:

- Left frame member
- Right frame member
- Top frame member
- Bottom frame member
- Glazing

Parametrics:

- Required reference planes
- Required dimensions
- Required constraints
- Overall family remains centred appropriately
- Width controls overall width
- Height controls overall height
- Frame Width controls frame geometry
- Frame Depth controls frame depth
- Glass Thickness controls glass thickness

FLEX TEST:

Changing Width must physically resize the family.

Changing Height must physically resize the family.

Changing Frame Width must physically modify the frame.

Changing Frame Depth must physically modify frame depth.

Changing Glass Thickness must physically modify glazing thickness.

Having a parameter in FamilyManager is NOT sufficient.

The parameter must actually drive the intended geometry.

==========================================================
WINDOW BENCHMARK FAILURE CONDITIONS
==========================================================

VALIDATION MUST FAIL if the result is:

- one generic rectangular extrusion
- one wall-like solid
- one rectangular panel
- a rectangular solid with only a void
- missing frame members
- missing glazing
- missing parameters
- parameters that exist but do not drive geometry
- dimensions that exist but do not constrain intended geometry
- geometry that breaks during parameter flexing

A Revit transaction successfully committing does NOT override these failure
conditions.

